-- title:
--   Proposition 6.12, Step 1 — the reference-curve argument excludes the negative cyclic difference
-- statement:
--   Let $m\\ge3$ and let $g$ satisfy the three axioms of Lemma 6.2 with $\\lambda=K+1$. Let $G$ be defined on an open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m!-m+1}$, parametrized by the external coordinates and total cyclic weight. Assume that $z_0\\in U$ is a differentiability point of $G$, that $G(z)$ is a common junction for every $z\\in U$, and that every coordinate of $G(z)$ exceeds $1$. Fix distinct candidates $c_i,c_j$.
--
--   Let $D=DG(z_0)$, $T=\\operatorname{im}D$, and let $C=\\operatorname{span}_{\\ge0}(T_{ij})$ be the cone generated by the transfer vectors (17). Then
--   $$
--   -(\\ell_i-\\ell_j)\\notin \\operatorname{cl}(C+T).
--   $$
--
--   This is exactly the exclusion (21), after the differentiability point has been selected by Rademacher's theorem and Proposition 6.11. The proof uses the reference curve $B_\\rho(t)$ and a finite sequence of rational ballot replacements, together with Lemma 6.10 and bounded manipulation potential.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The domain is `(ExtRanking m → ℝ) × ℝ`; profiles are vectors in `Ranking m → ℝ`, and Lean's norm on these finite function spaces is the sup norm. The nonnegative cone is represented as a submodule over $\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$. The hypotheses are precisely the outputs of Proposition 6.11 used in the source proof; no Lipschitz hypothesis is needed once differentiability at $z_0$ is supplied.
-- source:
--   Berker, Conitzer, Hartman, Liu, Oesterheld, How Many Votes Is a Lie Worth? Measuring Strategyproofness through Resource Augmentation, arXiv:2602.22838 (version of November 3, 2026, sha256 5a383ac5…), Proposition 6.12, Step 1, Eq. (21), pp.35–36 (proof continues p.37); uses Lemma 6.10, pp.32–33

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_GAxioms
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_TransferVectors
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_ell
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_unitVec
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_ExtRanking
import Definitions.Def_ManipPotential_IsCommonJunction

open scoped NNReal Pointwise

/-- Proposition 6.12, Step 1, Eq. (21) (Berker–Conitzer–Hartman–Liu–Oesterheld, *How Many Votes Is a Lie Worth? Measuring Strategyproofness through Resource Augmentation*, arXiv:2602.22838 (version of November 3, 2026, sha256 5a383ac5…), pp.35–36): at a differentiability point of the common-junction map, the negative cyclic difference is outside the closure of the sum of the nonnegative transfer cone and the image of the derivative. -/
theorem ManipPotential.proposition_6_12_step1_curve {m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (hm : 3 ≤ m) (K : ℕ)
    (g : (Ranking m → ℚ) → Fin m) (hg : GAxioms K g)
    (G : ((ExtRanking m → ℝ) × ℝ) → (Ranking m → ℝ))
    (U : Set ((ExtRanking m → ℝ) × ℝ)) (hUopen : IsOpen U)
    (z₀ : (ExtRanking m → ℝ) × ℝ) (hzU : z₀ ∈ U)
    (hzdiff : DifferentiableAt ℝ G z₀)
    (hJ : ∀ z ∈ U, IsCommonJunction g (G z))
    (hpos : ∀ z ∈ U, ∀ r, 1 < G z r) (i j : Fin m) (hij : i ≠ j) :
    let D : ((ExtRanking m → ℝ) × ℝ) →ₗ[ℝ] (Ranking m → ℝ) :=
      (fderiv ℝ G z₀).toLinearMap
    let T : Submodule ℝ (Ranking m → ℝ) := LinearMap.range D
    let C : Submodule ℝ≥0 (Ranking m → ℝ) :=
      Submodule.span ℝ≥0 (TransferVectors ((K : ℝ) + 1) i j)
    ¬ (-(unitVec (ell i) - unitVec (ell j)) ∈
      closure ((C : Set (Ranking m → ℝ)) + (T : Set (Ranking m → ℝ)))) := by sorry
